I have a complete picture. Here are my findings.

## Overview

This is a Spring PetClinic variant using OfficeFloor "function" pipelines. The owner request/response DTOs are **not hand-written Java** — they are generated at build time from the OpenAPI spec. The request DTO is `OwnerFieldsDto` and the response DTO is `OwnerDto`, both generated from `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml`. The flat `address` field lives on the `OwnerFields` schema, and the composed structured-address work you're planning will need to touch that schema plus the normalize/validate/build/mapper pipeline described below.

Note: the acceptance test suite **already contains scaffolding that anticipates your change** (structured `addressLine1`/`addressLine2`/`city`/`postcode` composed into `address`). See section 7 — those tests reference fields that do not yet exist in the schema, so they currently describe the target behavior.

---

## 1. The owner request DTO (schema source)

The request DTO `OwnerFieldsDto` is generated from the `OwnerFields` schema in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml` (lines 1991-2080). Verbatim schema:

```yaml
    OwnerFields:
      title: Owner fields
      description: Editable fields of a pet owner.
      type: object
      properties:
        firstName:
          ...
        lastName:
          ...
        address:
          title: Address
          description: The postal address of the pet owner.
          type: string
          minLength: 1
          maxLength: 255
          example: '110 W. Liberty St.'
        city:
          title: City
          description: The city of the pet owner.
          type: string
