            StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(digest.length * 2);
            for (byte b : digest) { sb.append(String.format("%02x", b)); }
            return sb.toString();
        } catch (NoSuchAlgorithmException e) {
            throw new IllegalStateException("SHA-256 not available", e);
        }
    }
}
```

Where the household hash is consumed:
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignOwnerHousehold.java` — `owner.setHouseholdId(Household.id(owner.getLastName(), owner.getPostcode()))`.
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignOwnerHouseholdSize.java` — counts owners sharing the household id (+1 for the new owner).
- Also referenced in `EnsureUniqueOwnerIdentity`, `AssignOwnerPossibleDuplicate`, and `OwnerIdentity` (the identity key `normalizedTelephone|email|householdId`).

---

## 4. Postcode validation logic

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/ValidateOwnerPostcode.java` — pipeline step. Full contents:

```java
public class ValidateOwnerPostcode {
    public void service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto request) throws InvalidOwnerFieldsException {
        String postcode = request.getPostcode();
        if (postcode == null || postcode.isBlank()) {
            return; // optional when absent
        }
        if (!PostcodeRange.isValidForCity(request.getCity(), postcode)) {
            throw new InvalidOwnerFieldsException(List.of("postcode"));
        }
    }
}
```

The rule implementation is `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/PostcodeRange.java`. Full contents:

```java
public final class PostcodeRange {

    private static final Map<String, int[]> BY_REGION = Map.of(
            "NSW", new int[] {2000, 2099},
            "VIC", new int[] {3000, 3099},
            "QLD", new int[] {4000, 4099});

    private PostcodeRange() {}

    public static String regionForPostcode(String postcode) {
        if (postcode == null || postcode.isBlank()) { return null; }
        int value;
        try { value = Integer.parseInt(postcode.trim()); }
        catch (NumberFormatException ex) { return null; }
        for (Map.Entry<String, int[]> entry : BY_REGION.entrySet()) {
            int[] range = entry.getValue();
            if (value >= range[0] && value <= range[1]) { return entry.getKey(); }
        }
        return null;
    }

    public static boolean isValidForCity(String city, String postcode) {
        int[] range = BY_REGION.get(CityRegion.locality(city));
        if (range == null) { return true; }
        int value = Integer.parseInt(postcode);
        return value >= range[0] && value <= range[1];
    }
}
```

The strict 4-digit format itself is enforced by the DTO pattern `^[0-9]{4}$` in the OpenAPI schema (checked by the bean `Validator` inside `ValidateOwnerFields`). `ValidateOwnerPostcode` only enforces the city-range relationship.

---

## 5. "Locality" logic that reads the address

Locality is derived from **postcode first, then city** — not from the free-text `address` string. The chain:

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/OwnerRegion.java`:
```java
public final class OwnerRegion {
    private OwnerRegion() {}
    public static String of(String postcode, String city) {
        String byPostcode = PostcodeRange.regionForPostcode(postcode);
        return byPostcode != null ? byPostcode : CityRegion.locality(city);
    }
}
```

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/util/CityRegion.java`:
```java
public final class CityRegion {
    public static final String UNKNOWN = "UNKNOWN";
    private static final Map<String, String> TABLE = Map.of(
            "Sydney", "NSW", "Melbourne", "VIC", "Brisbane", "QLD");
    private CityRegion() {}
    public static String locality(String city) {
        return city == null ? UNKNOWN : TABLE.getOrDefault(city, UNKNOWN);
    }
}
```

The response `locality` field is produced in the mapper (`OwnerMapper.locality(owner)`, section 8) as the REGION segment of the customerCode, falling back to `OwnerRegion.of(postcode, city)`. Region also feeds `AssignOwnerCustomerCode` (`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/AssignOwnerCustomerCode.java`) and the timezone via `RegionTimezone`. None of these read the `address` string; they read `city` and `postcode`.

---

## 6. Normalization logic applied to address/city/postcode

**Address** normalization is the only field-level normalization touching these three. Two files:

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/NormalizeOwnerAddress.java` — pipeline step that mutates the body in place:
```java
public class NormalizeOwnerAddress {
    public void service(@Val OwnerFieldsDto request) {
        String address = request.getAddress();
        if (address == null) { return; }
        request.setAddress(OwnerAddress.normalize(address));
    }
}
```

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/OwnerAddress.java` — the reusable normalizer (trim, collapse whitespace, upper-case, expand ST/RD/AVE):
```java
public final class OwnerAddress {

    private static final Map<String, String> ABBREVIATIONS = Map.of(
            "ST", "STREET", "RD", "ROAD", "AVE", "AVENUE");

    private OwnerAddress() {}

    public static String normalize(String address) {
        if (address == null) { return ""; }
        String collapsed = address.trim().replaceAll("\\s+", " ").toUpperCase(Locale.ROOT);
        if (collapsed.isEmpty()) { return ""; }
        String[] tokens = collapsed.split(" ");
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(collapsed.length());
        for (int i = 0; i < tokens.length; i++) {
            if (i > 0) { sb.append(' '); }
            sb.append(ABBREVIATIONS.getOrDefault(tokens[i], tokens[i]));
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```

This is the exact normalization your acceptance tests expect for the composed address (upper-case, collapse whitespace, `ST`->`STREET`). This is the natural place to compose `addressLine1 [+ " " + addressLine2]` and normalize the result.

There is **no** dedicated normalization for `city` or `postcode`. `city` is used verbatim as a map key in `CityRegion`; `postcode` is only `.trim()`-ed inside `PostcodeRange.regionForPostcode`. For reference, the sibling normalizers are `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/NormalizeOwnerEmail.java` and `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/NormalizeOwnerTelephone.java` (same @Val-mutation pattern).

---

## 7. Existing tests

There are **no JUnit unit tests** for the individual functions/utils. The only tests are black-box acceptance tests hitting `POST /api/owners` + `GET /api/owners/{id}`.

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceTest.java` — **directly relevant to your change** (it already assumes structured address composition exists). Full contents:

```java
/** address-structured: structured addressLine1/city/postcode is accepted and normalized (
 * rules: upper-case, collapse whitespace, expand ST->STREET), and 'address' is the composed
 * normalized string. The flat 'address' form stays accepted (backward-compatible). */
class AcceptanceTest extends AcceptanceBase {

	@Test
	void coreNormalizesAndComposesStructuredAddress() throws Exception {
		ObjectNode o = structuredOwner();
		o.put("addressLine1", "10 king st");
		o.remove("addressLine2");
		int id = createOwnerOk(o);
		getOwner(id).andExpect(jsonPath("$.addressLine1").value("10 KING STREET"))
				.andExpect(jsonPath("$.address").value("10 KING STREET"));
	}

	@Test
	void functionalityStillAcceptsFlatAddress() throws Exception {
		createOwner(ownerNode()).andExpect(status().is2xxSuccessful());
	}
}
```

Key expectations to satisfy: structured input `addressLine1` "10 king st" -> both `$.addressLine1` and `$.address` return "10 KING STREET" (normalized, ST expanded). Flat `address` payloads must keep working. Note it asserts `addressLine1` is echoed back in the response too, so `addressLine1` must be added to both `OwnerFields` (input) and effectively be a returned/derived field (`Owner` response).

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AcceptanceBase.java` — the base harness. Relevant payload builders:
- `ownerNode()` (lines 117-125): builds a valid flat-address owner (`o.put("address", uniqueAddress())`).
- `withPostcode(...)` (129-132): adds `"postcode": "2000"`.
- `structuredOwner()` (136-142) — **the structured-address builder your tests use**:
```java
protected ObjectNode structuredOwner() {
    ObjectNode o = ownerNode();
    o.remove("address");
    o.put("addressLine1", seq() + " Test Street");
    o.put("postcode", "2000");
    return o;
}
```
Note it removes flat `address` and sets `addressLine1` + `postcode` (no `addressLine2` unless a test adds it). It also has pinned reference tables for region/postcode/timezone (`CITY_REGION`, `REGION_TIMEZONE`, `REGION_POSTCODES`, lines 228-237) and helper `validPostcode(city)`.

`/home/daniel/sandbox/src/test/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/acceptance/AuditLogCapture.java` — audit-log test support (not address-related).

No tests exist specifically named for household hash, postcode util, locality, or normalization in isolation — they are all exercised only through the acceptance flow.

---

## 8. Response DTO and how `address` is returned

The response DTO `OwnerDto` is generated from the `Owner` schema in `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/resources/openapi.yml` (lines 2081-2268). It is `allOf: [OwnerFields, {read-only derived fields}]` — so it **inherits `address`, `city`, `postcode` directly from `OwnerFields`** and adds read-only derived fields: `id`, `displayName`, `initials`, `telephoneDisplay`, `locality`, `timezone`, `contactPreference`, `ageBand`, `customerCode`, `checkDigit`, `householdId`, `identityKey`, `namesakeCount`, `membershipNumber`, `membershipPoints`, `membershipLevel`, `bulkSignupWarning`, `possibleDuplicate`, `possibleDuplicateOf`, `pets`.

Because `address` is inherited from `OwnerFields`, the response `address` is simply the stored `Owner.address` string (the normalized value). There is no separate "compose address string" step on the response side today — the composition you plan must happen on the write path (normalize/build), storing a single flat `address` on the entity, which then round-trips out.

The response is built by:
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/RespondWithOwner.java`:
```java
public void service(@Val Owner owner, OwnerMapper ownerMapper, ObjectResponse<OwnerDto> response) {
    response.send(ownerMapper.toOwnerDto(owner));
}
```
- `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/mapper/OwnerMapper.java` — MapStruct mapper. `toOwnerDto(Owner)` maps `address`/`city`/`postcode` by name (implicit), and adds derived fields via `@Mapping(... expression=...)`. Relevant derived mappings:
  - `locality(owner)` -> customerCode REGION segment, else `OwnerRegion.of(postcode, city)`.
  - `timezone`, `contactPreference`, `identityKey`, `ageBand`, `displayName`, `initials`, `telephoneDisplay`, `membershipNumber`, `checkDigit`.
  - Inbound mapping `toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` (line 115, ignores `id`/`pets`) is where request `address` flows into the entity — MapStruct maps `address`->`address` by name. If you add `addressLine1/2` to the DTO but keep only `address` on the entity, MapStruct will ignore the new fields unless you compose them (e.g. in `NormalizeOwnerAddress`/`BuildOwner` or a custom mapping), and you'll likely need `@Mapping(target="addressLine1", ignore=true)` etc. or add them to the entity.

The entity build step is `/home/daniel/sandbox/src/main/java/org/springframework/samples/petclinic/rest/function/owner/BuildOwner.java` (`built.set(ownerMapper.toOwner(request))`).

---

## Summary of impact points for adding structured address

- Schema: `src/main/resources/openapi.yml` `OwnerFields` (add `addressLine1`, `addressLine2`; decide whether `address` becomes optional/derived). Response `Owner` inherits them automatically.
- Compose + normalize: `OwnerAddress.normalize` + `NormalizeOwnerAddress` (compose lines into flat `address`; the acceptance test expects `addressLine1` normalized identically and echoed back).
- Validation: `ValidateOwnerFields.requiredFields` (currently requires flat `address`; must accept structured form for backward-compat — flat still required per `functionalityStillAcceptsFlatAddress`).
- Entity/mapper: `Owner.java` (only has flat `address` column) and `OwnerMapper.toOwner(OwnerFieldsDto)` (name-based mapping; new fields need handling); response echoes `addressLine1` so it must be stored or re-derived.
- Unaffected by design but worth confirming: household hash (`Household`, keyed on lastName+postcode, not address), postcode validation (`ValidateOwnerPostcode`/`PostcodeRange`), and locality (`OwnerRegion`/`CityRegion`) all read `city`/`postcode`, not the address string.

